            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Alice starts the bathroom while Betty paints the kitchen for 2 hours; they then finish the bathroom together.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Alice is faster at painting the bathroom, and Betty is faster at painting the kitchen. Alice starts the bathroom while Betty paints the kitchen.\n\nAfter 2 hours, the kitchen is finished:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Bathroom fraction painted by Alice}&=\\frac{2}{3}\\\\\\text{Bathroom fraction remaining}&=1-\\frac{2}{3}\\\\\\text{Bathroom fraction remaining}&=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{align*}$$\nFollowing the common-time method, in 12 hours Alice can paint 4 bathrooms and Betty can paint 3. Together they paint 7 bathrooms in 12 hours, so the remaining third takes:\n$$\\begin{align*}\\text{Additional time}&=\\frac{12}{7}\\times\\frac{1}{3}\\\\\\text{Additional time}&=\\frac{4}{7}\\text{ hours}\\\\\\text{Total time}&=2+\\frac{4}{7}\\\\\\text{Total time}&=\\frac{18}{7}\\text{ hours}.\\end{align*}$$\nThis allocation is fastest because each painter starts on the room they paint more efficiently, and Betty joins Alice as soon as the kitchen is complete.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 130
